    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `_is_part_of_assignment_target`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_is_part_of_assignment_target` function checks whether a given variable node is part of the left-hand side of an assignment statement. This is important for understanding how variables are being used in the code, particularly in cases of destructuring assignment where variables can be unpacked from tuples or lists. The function takes a single parameter, `node`, which is an instance of `nodes.NodeNG` representing the variable in question. It returns a boolean value indicating whether the node is part of an assignment target. The function recursively checks the parent nodes to handle nested structures like tuples and lists. If the node's parent is an `Assign` or `AugAssign` statement, it checks if the node is among the targets or the target, respectively. If the parent is a `Tuple` or `List`, it calls itself recursively to continue the check up the tree. If none of these conditions are met, it returns `False`. The function does not handle any errors or exceptions; it simply returns `False` if the node is not part of an assignment target. There are no side effects; the function only queries the node and its parents without modifying any state.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions rai